 [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162164 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c9745592-4ec7-557a-9119-d1dcdcf23d32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a22ec54-0726-5bc9-a10c-e9f017685aa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162372 #edb/ref 1074 "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=3)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,3)}{{f}_{X}(2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =0.05+0.25 \\\\  & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,3)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(2)=0.30$ and $f(2,3)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,3)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.30} \\\\  & ≈0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cfbd164-6093-5409-a707-eefa23c3aa6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162374 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=2)=\\frac{P(X=2,Y=y)}{P(X=2)}=\\frac{f(2,y)}{{f}_{X}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in \\{0,1,2\\},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,0)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,1)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)}{{f}_{X}(2)}=\\frac{0.3}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163004 #edb/ref 1061 34578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7df83356-4053-5631-85dc-83521ae0811d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163011 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163011 #edb/ref 1061 34630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163011 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b56cb5f1-be37-5c19-a08d-76523e8ff1db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163011 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163012 #edb/ref 1061 34637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56d2a08f-a2e0-5602-b510-59f2481df9af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163065 #edb/ref 1061 37921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163065 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "000582c8-50ec-5bfc-b34a-c1cec0a03e2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163065 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163066 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163066 #edb/ref 1061 37922 #edb/ref 1759218