ero in the denominator, this formula assumes that ![](images/f4/f40e8e149f4e148b5042516dae366a5d17bdc00e1d6e39e27ddf30ece6801d6f.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/0f/0fa3deb4b6345d48bdeeb06c02ecd3d9bcf6a68cf517412b251a2bcf1e67d07f.svg) then ![](images/ad/ad17cd2272c82a9380977e2ef9ff99fb613681da7df20c06949db320c701bc4c.svg) is not defined by the usual conditional probability formula, since the denominator is zero. In this discrete setting, we therefore restrict the formula to values of ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) for which ![](images/1e/1ebc42b48118a4c62d7203d91e76ecc1a865f8d536cf145c82499ac8fd531cc7.svg)\n\nIn a similar manner, the conditional probability that ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) given that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) can be calculated as\n![](images/8c/8c8bdddcd02a672d59ded44b5281cf2f24236f88b2f494730bac998a760e9b0a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fd/fdb49440ceb7e11cd9ba0b0869a1e6d3565549969180830d1796db7dd00a2b96.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) This formula assumes that ![](images/f8/f8020df80e303daf760cd9bf4d8437cc08bc4b95b0ccf64bc7a43ccddc650f5f.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163288 #edb/ref 1065 5616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163288 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e69256ce-d99e-579e-a7f6-fbd459a410eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163288 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163325 #edb/ref 1064 "We can now define two more probability mass functions as follows:\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$,** denoted $f_{X|Y}(x|y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y = y) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, \\, Y = y)}{P(Y=y)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal mass function of $Y.$\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x,$** denoted $f_{Y|X}(y|x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{Y|X}(y|x) = P(Y = y \\,|\\, X = x) = \\dfrac{P(X = x,\\, Y = y)}{P(X=x)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal mass function of $X.$\n\n**Watch out!** The conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does *not* equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is, in general,\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)  \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $X$  and  $Y$ are independent, then knowing the value of one random variable does not affect the probability distribution of the other." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163325 #edb/ref 1065 6273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163325 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1cbdc2c5-105c-5f9a-b4f4-7e4651bdc789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163325 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditional Probability Mass Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e911b538-24df-51a8-a134-2c4a130edca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363280 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2)=P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(4,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(2,2)+f(4,2) \\\\  & =0.4+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,2)=0.15.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(2)=0.55$ and $f(4,2)=0.15$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,2) & =P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=2) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.55} \\\\  & ≈0.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363281 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "945c9fdc-f2f4-578d-8093-3c96735abd64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363282 #edb/ref 1080 "0.33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363282 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363282 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "772f1af4-3e1a-5d24-b6f2-84e08728280e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363283 #edb/ref 1080 "0.27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363283 #edb/re